From 375ed13ae71b0f445c672376da9d4dbf57f2193d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 20:56:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 078/113] rust: usb: add typed endpoints and a revocable I/O
 window

A driver-`Bound` USB interface is not on its own proof that a transfer may
be issued: the USB core forbids I/O outside the window that opens after a
successful probe/resume/reset-resume and must close again before
disconnect, suspend or pre_reset returns. The previous transfer stack
enabled every operation on `Interface<Bound>` and offered an unsafe
`as_bound()` so a driver could assert that property by hand at each call
site, which is the layering Oliver Neukum and Danilo Krummrich objected to
in the v2 review.

Replace it with two abstractions:

`IoWindow` represents the narrower I/O-permitted state. A driver opens one
during probe and closes it in disconnect; `close()` refuses new tokens,
cancels the URBs of any queue registered against it, and blocks until every
outstanding token is dropped, so no transfer can be in flight outside the
window. The single documented `unsafe fn new()` obligation replaces an
unsafe assertion at every transfer. Transfers live on the `Io` token rather
than on the interface, so they cannot be reached without it.

`Endpoint<K>` is looked up in the interface's active alternate setting and
checked against the descriptor, so a bulk OUT method cannot be pointed at
an interrupt IN endpoint. `set_alternate_setting()` takes the interface
number from the bound interface itself, so a driver can no longer retarget
a sibling interface of a composite device.

Several defects found in the same review are fixed on the way: queues
reject zero depth instead of dividing by zero, construction can no longer
leak the device reference or an URB when a later allocation fails, a failed
URB re-submission is tracked rather than leaving a later wait blocked on a
completion that can never fire, and a non-zero sub-millisecond timeout is
rounded up to 1 ms instead of truncating to the helpers' "wait forever".

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-0
Assisted-by: Codex:gpt-5
Signed-off-by: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
